    "lawCode": "Amd §35, Work Comp L",
    "lawSection": "Workers' Compensation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4839A\n\nSPONSOR: AKSHAR\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the workers' compensation law, in\nrelation to reporting by the workers' compensation board and appoint-\nments to the advisory board; and providing for the repeal of such\nprovisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis legislation would transfer responsibility of generating and promul-\ngating the \"Safety Net Report\", which is an annual report chronicling\nworkers' compensation claimants who have been awarded Permanent Partial\nDisability (PPD) Non-Scheduled Loss (NSL) status, from the Department of\nLabor to the WCB.\n\nAn advisory board shall assist the WCB in generating the Safety Net\nReport. The advisory board shall consist of seven members: a represen-\ntative from organized labor; a representative of the business community;\none member on recommendation by the majority leader of the senate; one\nmember on recommendation of the speaker of the assembly; two members at\nthe governor's discretion, and; the commissioner of the department of\nlabor.\n\nFurther, this legislation requires that the Safety Net Report be submit-\nted annually in December, to the governor, speaker of the assembly,\nmajority leader of the senate and the chairs of the labor, ways and\nmeans and finance committees of the senate and assembly.\n\n \nREASONS FOR SUPPORT:\n\nSection 35 of the Workers' Compensation Law (WCL) was amended in 2007 to\ncreate 'Safety Net and Return to Work' provisions. Section 35 required\nthe commissioner of labor to release an annual report detailing return\nto work rates for PPD NSL claimants. The Commissioner of Labor (Commis-\nsioner) has been tasked with the responsibility of issuing the Safety\nNet Report.\n\nThe Commissioner has not released a Safety Net Report since 2010 despite\nthe statutory mandate.\n\nOne of the central tenants of workers' compensation is to assist in\nfacilitating an injured workers' return to gainful employment.  Without\nthe Safety Net Report, it is nearly impossible to assess whether the\ncurrent system is achieving its goals relative to injured workers and if\nnot, how future legislation could target critical deficiencies. The\nCommissioner's failure to comply with a legislative mandate is plainly\nunacceptable, and serves as an impediment to meaningful workers compen-\nsation reform.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT:\n\nTBD\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
